WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.493
What should a detector do when it encounters something outside its training categories?

2
00:00:05.179 --> 00:00:09.576
I led a research group exploring unknown-object detection for autonomous driving.

3
00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:17.016
We reproduced Spatial Temporal Unknown Distillation, or STUD, using Python and Detectron2.

4
00:00:17.702 --> 00:00:23.576
My work combined research planning, environment setup, data preparation, and inspection of detection

5
00:00:23.576 --> 00:00:24.260
results.

6
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:31.423
The data work covered BDD one hundred K, COCO twenty seventeen, and YouTube VIS.

7
00:00:31.423 --> 00:00:36.416
Adapting formats and selected categories helped connect these sources to the reproduced baseline.

8
00:00:37.983 --> 00:00:41.398
The midterm slides reproduce this example from the STUD paper.

9
00:00:42.084 --> 00:00:47.064
Giraffes are marked unknown, while a rock is labelled train at seventy-one percent confidence.

10
00:00:47.751 --> 00:00:52.282
This is a paper example and a single detection score, not our measured accuracy.

11
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.932
Further paper examples show unknown elephants and unfamiliar indoor objects.

12
00:00:58.618 --> 00:01:01.948
The elephant scene also contains a false pedestrian detection.

13
00:01:02.635 --> 00:01:07.356
These figures illustrate what to inspect; they are not newly generated outputs from our reproduced

14
00:01:07.356 --> 00:01:07.967
system.

15
00:01:09.517 --> 00:01:15.645
The STUD paper reports an A U R O C of eighty-five point six seven percent on COCO, and seventy-five

16
00:01:15.645 --> 00:01:17.689
point six seven on nu Images.

17
00:01:18.374 --> 00:01:22.444
These are reference benchmarks for its BDD one hundred K trained detector.

18
00:01:23.132 --> 00:01:25.490
They are not final scores from my project.

19
00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:30.295
Higher A U R O C means better separation.

20
00:01:30.981 --> 00:01:34.601
Under the paper convention, in-distribution detections are positive.

21
00:01:35.289 --> 00:01:40.131
F P R at ninety-five is the share of unknown detections wrongly accepted as known when known

22
00:01:40.131 --> 00:01:43.133
detections reach ninety-five percent true positive rate.

23
00:01:44.683 --> 00:01:49.950
The midterm plan proposed adverse-weather shares of twenty-five, fifty, seventy-five, and one

24
00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.928
hundred percent, keeping the training-set size fixed.

25
00:01:53.614 --> 00:01:57.198
These are proposed experiment settings, not measured improvements.

26
00:01:58.783 --> 00:02:04.778
The contribution is a documented research process: reproduction, data adaptation, and failure

27
00:02:04.778 --> 00:02:05.572
analysis.

28
00:02:06.258 --> 00:02:07.743
PAIR remained exploratory.

29
00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:11.869
Explore the full case study for the evidence and planned next steps.
